<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  com.twinvpn.ksd — the KS-19 BOOT ARTIFACT. Installed at
  /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.twinvpn.ksd.plist.

  Authority: ADR-0016 §11.2 (the macOS component table), §11.5 (the macOS
  durability row), PS-7, PS-8; ADR-0012 KS-19, KS-20, KS-20a, §11.6's macOS row.

  ============================================================ WHAT ksd IS NOT

  ADR-0016 §11.2, "macOS, normatively", verbatim:

    "The system extension is the authority; the `LaunchDaemon` `ksd` is *not* a
     general-purpose privileged helper and MUST NOT accept any request other
     than (a) apply the boot anchor and (b) the unblock command's local,
     admin-authenticated invocation."

  and amendment PS-22 restates it as a table:

    | `ksd`, the LaunchDaemon | the KS-19 boot anchor, and the unblock
    | command's local admin-authenticated invocation | no core, no keys, no
    | network sockets, no management interface |

  So this job has NO network access, NO core, NO key access and NO management
  interface. It runs, applies /etc/twinvpn/pf.anchor, READS BACK what pfctl
  says is loaded, and exits.

  THE AUTHORITY IS THE SYSTEM EXTENSION, and there is no second LaunchDaemon.
  Wave 2 shipped a `com.twinvpn.twinvpnd` beside this one that hosted the core
  and served the MI; ownership.md §9.6 X-7 closed that as a defect and its plist
  is deleted. If you are here looking for it, that is why it is not.

  ADR-0016 §11.5's macOS row says why this exists as a second privileged
  component at all: "a sysext can be deactivated by the user, and the boot
  artifact must not be able to be."

  ================================================ PS-7: THE PACKAGE OWNS THIS

  "installed by the package, modified only by atomic replace under ADMINISTER;
   the authority MUST NOT rewrite it as a runtime action, and MUST NOT be a
   prerequisite for it to apply."

  Both halves are structural here. The authority never opens this file (it has
  no code path that does), and this job does not depend on the authority: it
  runs at load, before systemextensionsd has activated anything, and succeeds
  or fails on its own.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- ADR-0016 §11.2's macOS row names this component `com.twinvpn.ksd`. The
         label is also the launchd service name, so `launchctl print
         system/com.twinvpn.ksd` is how an operator inspects it. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.twinvpn.ksd</string>

    <!--
      The binary. ADR-0016 §11.2 says ksd "is installed by
      SMAppService.daemon(plistName:) on macOS 13+ and by SMJobBless on macOS
      11-12, in both cases signed with the same Team ID and embedded in the app
      bundle."

      Those two installation routes want DIFFERENT keys, and this file carries
      the pkg-installer form:

        - pkg installer (this file, /Library/LaunchDaemons/): ProgramArguments
          with an ABSOLUTE path, as below.
        - SMAppService.daemon(plistName:) (macOS 13+): the plist lives at
          Contents/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.twinvpn.ksd.plist INSIDE the app
          bundle and names the executable with `BundleProgram`, a path relative
          to the bundle root (e.g. Contents/MacOS/twinvpn-ksd). launchd resolves
          it against the registering bundle.

      UNVERIFIED: whether a single plist can carry both keys and be accepted by
      both routes. This domain has not tested it. The installer ships this
      absolute-path form; the SMAppService variant is a separate build product
      and is NOT in this wave.
    -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/Library/Application Support/TwinVPN/twinvpn-ksd</string>
        <string>--apply-boot-anchor</string>
    </array>

    <!-- KS-19: "the rule set that covers the interval between the network stack
         coming up and the agent starting". RunAtLoad is what puts the deny
         before the first packet the host can emit. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      ADR-0016 §11.5's macOS supervisor row, verbatim: "launchd for ksd:
      KeepAlive={SuccessfulExit:false}, subject to launchd's 10 s throttle."

      SuccessfulExit=false means: restart on a CRASH, do not restart on a clean
      exit. That is exactly right for a job that applies a ruleset and exits 0.
    -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <!--
      launchd's throttle. ADR-0016 §11.5 names 10 s for this job.

      **This does NOT discharge PS-9 or PS-10.** The same row is explicit:
      "launchd has no burst limit, so the authority maintains its OWN durable
      restart counter (S-40) and latches quarantine itself." A ThrottleInterval
      spaces restarts out; it never gives up, so it cannot be the crash-loop
      containment those rules require. The counter that can is the AUTHORITY's
      — the system extension's — not this job's. This plist must not be read as
      if the key below were the containment.

      And note what this job's KeepAlive is actually for: SuccessfulExit=false
      restarts a CRASH and not a clean exit, and this job exits 0 once the
      anchor is confirmed loaded. A restart here means the apply or the
      read-back failed, which is the case worth retrying.
    -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>10</integer>

    <!--
      Runs as root. ADR-0016 §11.2's macOS row: "`ksd`: root, no network, no
      core, no key access."

      There is deliberately no UserName key dropping to `_twinvpn`: pfctl needs
      root, and this job's entire surface is one pfctl invocation. The
      mitigation for running as root is not a privilege drop, it is that the
      job is tiny, has no input channel, and exits.
    -->
    <key>UserName</key>
    <string>root</string>

    <key>GroupName</key>
    <string>wheel</string>

    <!-- Background: no UI, low scheduling priority, no App Nap participation.
         `Background` also disables the automatic throttling `Adaptive` applies. -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>

    <!--
      No network. ADR-0016 §11.2's macOS row again: ksd has "no network".
      launchd has no key that revokes networking, so this is not enforceable
      from the plist — it is a property of the BINARY, which links no socket
      code. Recorded here because a reader looking for the enforcement will not
      find it and should know it is elsewhere rather than missing.
    -->

    <!--
      ADR-0016 §11.9's hardening intent, as far as launchd expresses it.
      HardResourceLimits/SoftResourceLimits cap what a compromised ksd could do;
      the numbers are deliberately small because the job opens one file and
      spawns pfctl.
    -->
    <key>SoftResourceLimits</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NumberOfFiles</key>
        <integer>64</integer>
        <key>NumberOfProcesses</key>
        <integer>16</integer>
    </dict>

    <!--
      Diagnostics. ADR-0015: never a tunnel payload, a key or a token. This job
      handles none of those; what it writes is the pfctl exit status and the
      reason_code for a refusal.

      /var/log/twinvpn/ is created 0750 root:wheel by the installer. StandardOut
      is deliberately /dev/null: a boot-path job that writes to a log on every
      boot is a log that nobody reads and a disk that eventually fills.
    -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/dev/null</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/twinvpn/ksd.err.log</string>

    <!--
      ADR-0016 Q10: a privileged process MUST NOT inherit a search path, preload
      variable or plugin directory that could supply it executable code. launchd
      gives a daemon a minimal environment already; PATH is pinned to the two
      system directories that hold pfctl so that nothing else can be found under
      that name.
    -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
    </dict>

    <!--
      No MachServices, no Sockets, no WatchPaths, no StartInterval. ADR-0016
      §11.2 forbids ksd accepting "any request other than (a) apply the boot
      anchor and (b) the unblock command's local, admin-authenticated
      invocation" — and (b) is discharged by `twinvpn-unblock`, a SEPARATE
      package-owned privileged binary at /usr/local/sbin/twinvpn-unblock, rather
      than by a request channel into this job.

      ADR-0017 MI-12 names `ksd` as the SERVING component for the unblock on
      macOS, and this build reads that as "the same package, the same privilege
      class, the same uninstall" rather than "an IPC endpoint on this daemon" —
      because MI-12's own first sentence is that the command "MUST NOT depend on
      the management interface, on the agent process, or on any MI channel", and
      a Mach service on this job would be a channel with exactly the
      availability problem the rule exists to avoid. Reported to the integration
      lead as a READING, not a resolution: shells/macos/README.md §7.

      Declaring an unused MachServices key here would in any case create the
      request channel §11.2 forbids.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
